== Historical record ==
By the way, in the twelfth volume of the "Nihon Shoki," [[Emperor Richū|Emperor Richu]] floats a two-pronged boat on Iware City Isoike Pond. <ref>『日本書紀』履中天皇3年11月6日条</ref>

=== The Yamato military fields and warehouses ===
Similarly, Volume 11 of the "Shoki" tells the following story:

This is the story of how Agoko remade a giant tree that had washed up on the Oi River into a boat. At this time, Agoko was called "Yamato no Agoko."

The Crown Prince, who was filled with suspicion, became suspicious of Agogo's true intentions and tried to kill him. So Agoko felt humbled and offered his younger sister, Hinohime, as a tribute, and was spared the death penalty. From this time on, Yamato no Agoko began to offer court ladies as tribute. <ref>『日本書紀』履中天皇即位前紀条</ref>

According to Volume 12 of the "Shoki," Prince Sumiyoshi Nakatsu and Yamato Naokogo were on close terms. Therefore, when Prince Naka rebelled against the Crown Prince, Prince Izahowake (later Emperor Richu), presumably in the year 399, he tried to side with Prince Naka and gathered several hundred elite soldiers at a place called "Kakihami no Kurusu" (said to be Kurusu-go, {{ill|10=ja|11=忍海郡|Oshikai County}} ) and waited for the Crown Prince. However, when he realized that the Crown Prince's troops were more numerous than he had expected, he changed his attitude.

=== Driftwood of the Oi River ===
Another part of the Richū Sokui Chronicle states that the Crown Prince, on his way to {{Ill|Isonokami Shrine|en|Isonokami Shrine}}, encounters a young girl at the foot of Mt. Asuka and asks her about the safety of the journey.{{Quote frame|In the summer of May of the 62nd year (estimated to be 374 AD), the governor of Tōtōmi Province reported, "There is a large tree that flowed from the {{ill|Ōi River|en}}, bending and stopping at the river's curve. Its size is around ten tōdaki (approximately 300 meters). Initially singular, it splits into two at the ends." At that time, Yamato no Ataī Agoko was dispatched to construct a ship from it. Thus, navigating from the Southern Sea, it arrived at Naniwa Port, where it was dedicated as a royal ship.<!--六十二年の夏五月（推定[[374年]]）に、[[遠淡海国造|遠江国司]]<small>（とほつあふみのくに の みこともち）</small>表上言<small>（まう）</small>さく、「大<small>（おほ）</small>きなる樹<small>（き）</small>有りて、[[大井川|大井河]]<small>（おほゐがは）</small>より流れて、河<small>（かは）</small>曲<small>（くま）</small>り停<small>（とどま）</small>れり。其大きさ十囲<small>（とうだき＝30尺、およそ300メートル）</small>。本<small>（もと）</small>は壱<small>（ひとつ）</small>にして末は両<small>（またまた）</small>なり」とまうす。時に倭直吾子籠<small>（やまとのあたひあごこ）</small>を遣<small>（つかは）</small>して船に造らしむ。而<small>（しかう）</small>して南海<small>（みなみのみち）</small>より運<small>（めぐら）</small>して、難波津<small>（なにはのつ）</small>に将<small>（ゐ）</small>て来<small>（きた）</small>りて、御船<small>（みふね）</small>に充<small>（あ）</small>てつ。--><ref>『日本書紀』仁徳天皇62年5月条</ref>}}This episode is about a massive log that had drifted to the Ōigawa River, which Agoko then remade into a ship. At this time, Agoko was referred to as "Yamato no Atai."

Incidentally, in Volume 12 of the "Nihon Shoki," it is mentioned that Emperor Nintoku floated a bifurcated boat (a boat split into two parts) in the Iware Market Isopond.

=== Prince Sumiyoshi's Rebellion ===
The fourteenth volume of the "Shoki" tells the story of Emperor Yuryaku, who, in a fit of rage after a hunt at Mimase, killed Otsu no Umakai with the sword, and his mother, Empress {{ill|Oshisaka no Ōnakatsuhime|en}}, presenting the Wa court lady "Hinohime" to settle the matter. This conversation led to the establishment of {{ill|10=ja|11=宍人部|the Shishihitobe}} (the branch of the Shishihito clan), and the appointment of three members, including Kashiwadeno no Omi Nagano. In addition, the “Oyamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko Agoko no Sukune” presented Sahonoko Toriwake to the Shishihitobe, and “the Ministers of the Imperial Court, Tomo no Miyatsuko and Kuni no Miyatsuko, followed suit and continued to offer tribute.” <ref>『日本書紀』雄略天皇2年10月6日条</ref>{{Quote frame|"I hear through the grapevine that an extraordinary event has occurred to the Crown Prince. Preparing soldiers, we stand ready to offer assistance."<!--「伝<small>（つて）</small>に聞く、皇太子<small>（ひつぎのみこ）</small>、非常之事<small>（おもほえぬこと）</small>有<small>（ま）</small>しますと。助けまつらむとして兵<small>（つはもの）</small>を備<small>（そな）</small>へて待ちたてまつる」-->}}As a character in the " [[Nihon Shoki]] ," he appears in relatively many places.

Volume 11 of the Nihon Shoki records a dispute over the Yamato military fields and warehouses between {{ill|10=ja|11=額田大中彦皇子|Prince Nukata no Onakahiko}}  and the {{ill|Tuntian|lt=military farm|en|10=ja|11=屯田}} administrator, {{ill|10=ja|11=淤宇宿禰|Ounosukune}}, which took place after the death of the Emperor in what is [[Emperor Ōjin|believed]] to be 310 (the 41st year of the reign of Emperor Ojin) and {{ill|10=ja|11=屯倉|the Imperial Throne}} was in a state of uncertainty. When Prince Nukata no Oonakahiko unilaterally confiscated his land, Ousukune appealed to Uji no Wakairoko and then to Osazaki no Mikoto (later [[Emperor Nintoku]] ), and it was discovered that Agoko, who was currently stationed in Karakuni, knew about the Wa military fields and military storehouses. Following the advice of Ootori-no-Mikoto, Ousukune traveled day and night to Korea with Awaji fishermen and returned with the child's basket.

Akogo testified that the "military farms (屯田) and granaries (屯倉) of Wa" were passed down to Emperor Keikō, who was [[Emperor Keikō|the crown prince]] during the reign of [[Emperor Suinin]], saying, "Generally, the military farms (屯田)  of Wa are the military farms (屯田)  of the current emperor. Even if one is the son of the emperor, he cannot be in charge unless he is the emperor's son," and that they were under the control of the emperor of the time. This caused Prince Nukata-Onakahiko to reflect on his actions, and the land management issue was resolved in Ousukune's favor.

The [[Kojiki]] makes no mention of such circumstances.

=== Presentation from the Shishitobe ===
It is said that the Hihime who appears here and Sumiyoshi's younger sister, Hinohime, are the same person, and that there may be some confusion in the legends.

The above description needs to be considered in conjunction with historical facts uncovered by archaeology, which show that the chiefs who became the ancestors of the Izumo no Omi clan in the first half of the 5th century were deeply involved with the Yamato government and engaged in negotiations with the Korean Peninsula. Furthermore, it is necessary to look at the connections with {{ill|10=ja|11=淡路島|the Awaji Island}} fishermen group and the relationship with the {{ill|Nomi no Sukune|en}} legend.

